  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'd like help creating a running plan for my first half marathon. I want to prescribe the first four weeks now, then extend the plan after feedback.",
      "sequence": 1
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely—we can map out the build to your first half marathon, prescribe the first four weeks, then adjust and extend it based on how you’re responding.\n\n**What’s the race date, or your target timeframe if you haven’t entered one yet?**",
      "sequence": 2
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Start my plan on 2026-10-12. My half marathon is on 2027-01-03, an A-priority goal race. I have no other races planned. My goal is to finish comfortably, without a target finish time.",
      "sequence": 3
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That gives us **12 weeks**, starting Monday 12 October and finishing with your A-priority half marathon on 3 January. We’ll prescribe **12 October–8 November** first, then extend after your feedback.\n\nTo set a sensible starting load, **what has your running looked like over the last few weeks—runs per week, approximate weekly distance, and your longest recent run?** Kilometres or miles are both fine.",
      "sequence": 4
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "For the last six weeks I have run three times per week, about 22 km total per week. My longest recent run is 9 km, which felt comfortable. Running is the only sport this plan should train.",
      "sequence": 5
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That’s a useful starting base: **three runs and 22 km per week**, with a comfortable 9 km long run. I’ve saved those details and kept the plan running-only.\n\n**Would you like to stay at three runs per week, or build toward four—and which weekdays can you run?**",
      "sequence": 6
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I want exactly three runs per week.\nI can run only on Tuesdays, Thursdays and Sundays. Sundays are best for my long run. All other weekdays are unavailable. I have up to 60 minutes on Tuesdays and Thursdays and up to 100 minutes on Sundays.",
      "sequence": 7
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Saved: **exactly three runs per week**, on Tuesdays, Thursdays and Sundays, with Sunday as your long run. I’ll keep weekday sessions within **60 minutes** and Sunday runs within **100 minutes**, leaving all other days free.\n\n**Do you have a recent race result—such as a 5K or 10K—or a known threshold pace?** Include roughly when it was recorded; this will help set appropriate training intensities.",
      "sequence": 8
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I ran an all-out, flat 5 km race in 28 minutes on 2026-09-25 in fair conditions. That reflects my current fitness.",
      "sequence": 9
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I’ve recorded your **28:00 5K from 25 September** as your first running fitness benchmark. Your training zones now apply from today across your plans.\n\nYour calculated **easy pace is roughly 6:52–7:44/km**. Treat that as a guide, not a requirement—easy runs should feel conversational, and it’s fine to slow down for hills, heat or fatigue.\n\nBefore I prescribe the first four weeks, **are there any current or recent injuries, health concerns, or other constraints that could affect your running?**",
      "sequence": 10
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm 35, have no current injuries or health restrictions, and can run on flat roads and paths. I prefer distances in kilometres.",
      "sequence": 11
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Your **first four weeks are saved as a draft**, covering **12 October–8 November 2026**.\n\n### First four weeks\nAll runs are easy and conversational.\n\n| Week | Tuesday | Thursday | Sunday long run | Total |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 12–18 Oct | 6 km | 7 km | 9 km | **22 km** |\n| 19–25 Oct | 6 km | 7 km | 10 km | **23 km** |\n| 26 Oct–1 Nov | 6 km | 7 km | 11 km | **24 km** |\n| 2–8 Nov — cutback | 5 km | 6 km | 8 km | **19 km** |\n\nMonday, Wednesday, Friday and Saturday are rest days.\n\n**How to run these sessions**\n- Use your race-based easy zone—approximately **6:52–7:44/km**—as a guide, not a pace requirement. You should be able to speak in full sentences.\n- Start gently for the first kilometre and ease down over the final kilometre.\n- Keep Tuesday and Thursday within **60 minutes**, and Sunday within **100 minutes**. Shorten the distance if necessary.\n- Increase the long run only if the previous week felt comfortable and recovery was normal; otherwise repeat the previous comfortable distance.\n\nThe fourth week deliberately reduces mileage so you can absorb the training.\n\n**Next steps:** Share how the runs feel, actual durations, any discomfort and next-day recovery—particularly after the long runs. We’ll use that feedback to prescribe the next block.\n\nValidation flagged the expected gaps **after 8 November**, which we’re intentionally leaving unprescribed, plus the need for you to **review and confirm the brief in the app before locking**. The four-week prescription is complete; the draft remains unlocked.",
      "sequence": 12
    }
  ]
